{"content":"h07.\n\nTracing a browser request for another user's profile image through to stored data:\n\n**HTTP Handler \u0026 Route:**\nserveUserAvatar — server/router/fileserver/fileserver.go:168\n\n**First hop: Fetch user by username**\ngetUserByUsername — server/router/fileserver/fileserver.go:715\n\n**Second hop: Query store layer**\nStore.GetUser — store/user.go:148\n\n**Third hop: Check cache, then delegate to driver**\nStore.ListUsers — store/user.go:136\n\n**Fourth hop: Database driver interface**\nDriver.ListUsers — store/driver.go:47\n\n**Fifth hop: Concrete database implementation (SQLite example)**\nsqlite.DB.ListUsers — store/db/sqlite/user.go:88\n\n**Final hop: SQL query to stored data**\nSELECT query from user table with avatar_url column — store/db/sqlite/user.go:151-165\n\nThe request flow is:\n1. Browser → GET `/file/users/:identifier/avatar`\n2. Handler parses username, checks authentication/authorization\n3. Calls `getUserByUsername()` which invokes `Store.GetUser()`\n4. `Store.GetUser()` checks in-memory user cache, then calls `Store.ListUsers()` if miss\n5. `ListUsers()` delegates to driver implementation (e.g., `sqlite.DB.ListUsers()`)\n6. Driver executes SQL `SELECT ... FROM user WHERE username = ?` which retrieves `avatar_url` column\n7. Avatar data (base64-encoded image) is extracted and served via `parseDataURI()` and `c.Blob()`","iteration":11,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
